In this lesson we'll look at how digital tools can run thousands of chance experiments in seconds, and why bigger samples give results that match our predictions more closely. Instead of flipping a coin a few times by hand, you can use a spreadsheet, an online die-roller, or a bit of code to model the experiment, say rolling a die or tossing a coin, and let the computer do it over and over, hundreds or even thousands of times. You then count how often your event happens, work out the relative frequency, and compare it to the probability you predicted. Now, your observed frequency will almost never match the prediction exactly, and there are two main reasons. First, there's just random variation, a fair coin can still land heads sixty times out of a hundred, that's just luck. Second, there's the sample size. Small samples tend to bounce around a lot, but as you collect more and more trials the gap between what you observed and what you predicted usually shrinks. A handy rule of thumb is that if you multiply the number of trials by ten, the typical gap shrinks by roughly the square root of ten, so ten times more data gives you about three times more accuracy. That's why statisticians love big samples. Now let's see it in action.